          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. The rest of her turn intensifies that avowal through comparisons claiming incomparable filial love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The focal expression answers Lear's request for a comparative declaration of present love; it does not itself create, renew, or invoke a commitment by Goneril to perform duties or remain bound in the future. The filial comparison describes the magnitude of her claimed love rather than affirming an obligation. Lear's subsequent territorial grant is his separate act, not undertaking force in Goneril's words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an elaborate, controlled ceremonial answer rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its heightened rhetoric intensifies an avowal but does not establish a spontaneous exclamation or involuntary response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love. Although it is strategically responsive within Lear's public contest for bounty, that setting supplies a motive and rhetorical function rather than a distinct residual speech-act mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most so that he may extend his largest bounty accordingly. Goneril, called on first, addresses Lear with an expansive declaration of filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established in the focal expression or its surrounding turn: she claims an exceptionally strong present love but specifies no commitment or obligation. Lear treats her answer as satisfactory and immediately grants territory to her and Albany's line, but this is uptake of her declaration within his love test and a separate act by Lear, not acceptance of a promise from Goneril.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognised as the opening scene of William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence and does not import later plot events.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage establishes the public, competitive circumstances and the avowing form of Goneril's words, but it does not by itself settle whether her professed affection is sincere, what private intentions she has, or whether any unstated relational commitment is intended.\"}"
